title: Builder
manual: structure/#c2-edit

Modify the structure shown in the view by clicking on it: add or remove atoms and
fragments, change the number of hydrogens, create or remove bonds, and edit distances,
angles and dihedrals. Choose a tool from the palette; the panel below explains what a
click in the view does and how to stop. Undo in the Edit menu reverts a change.

# Tools
- Add: "Add Atoms" opens the periodic table; the element is placed with the selected local
  geometry (linear, bent, tetrahedral, ...), and clicking an atom replaces it, keeping the
  non-hydrogen substituents. "Add Fragments" opens the fragment library; clicking an atom
  bonds the fragment to it (ligands) or replaces the atom, and clicking empty space places
  the fragment there. The "Recent:" row re-arms an entry placed before.
- Valence: "Change Valence" adds or removes hydrogens on the clicked atom.
- Remove: "Remove Atoms" deletes the clicked atoms and their terminal hydrogens; "Trim
  Branches" also deletes every fragment left disconnected.
- Bonds: "Create Bonds" bonds two atoms clicked one after the other, changing only the
  connectivity; "Create Bonds (-H)" also removes a terminal hydrogen from each; "Remove
  Bonds" removes the clicked bond; "Bond Order" cycles it through single, double, triple,
  aromatic and dashed.
- Edit: select two, three or four atoms in the view, then press the button to change
  their distance, angle or dihedral, choosing which atoms move. "Apply" commits and
  "Revert" restores the starting value.
- Symmetry: moves the atoms onto their symmetry positions exactly; the point or space
  group is shown next to it.
- Relax: relaxes the geometry to the nearest minimum with the chosen force field until
  the maximum force drops below "Fmax (eV/Å)". Only the atoms selected in the view move;
  with no selection, all of them do.
- "Rebond" recomputes the connectivity, discarding the bonds created or removed by hand;
  "Restore" reloads the original geometry, discarding every edit.
- The "Atoms", "Cell", "Bonds", "Symmetry" and "Dynamics" buttons open those windows.

# Notes
- Errors from a tool appear in red at the bottom of the window.
